From: Codex <codex@openai.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ctrl: reconcile agent reachability after topology change

IEEE1905 now emits a normal Topology Notification when an established
neighbor expires, but the controller routes every notification through the
client-association path.  A neighbor-only notification has no BSSID and is
discarded before Unified Wi-Fi Mesh can react.  The stale agent consequently
remains in the active topology indefinitely.

Route notifications without a Client Association Event through a synchronized
controller radio and start a bounded, one-shot Topology Query probe of the
affected agent.  The AL node is only a transport listener and never reaches the
topology-synchronized protocol state required by the configuration handler.
Any valid EasyMesh frame refreshes that agent's controller-owned last-seen state.
If an agent does not answer within ten seconds, suppress it from the active
topology without deleting its runtime or persistent identity.  The first
valid returning frame makes the same logical device reachable and publishes
it again.  This also handles re-parenting: an agent reachable through any path
answers its direct controller probe and is never aged out.  The local AL-SAP
indication carries the expired neighbor in its endpoint metadata; the Ethernet
multicast notification remains a standard IEEE 1905 frame.

The probe is triggered only by the IEEE1905 topology-change notification; it
is not a periodic poll and uses no wmediumd or WebUI knowledge.
The colocated Agent is exempt because it is a local controller data-model
identity rather than a remote radio endpoint and does not answer this probe.

